<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Li Qingzhao</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1084–c. 1155</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Yi'an Jushi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Li Qingzhao was a Chinese poet and essayist of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a>, considered one of the greatest poets in Chinese history. Born in 1084 in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a> to a family of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scholar-officials">scholar-officials</a>, she was known in elite circles by 1101, when she married Zhao Mingcheng, with whom she collected books, inscriptions and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calligraphy">calligraphy</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jin-song-wars">Jin–Song wars</a> the couple fled south after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jingkang-incident">fall of Kaifeng</a> in 1127; Zhao died in 1129, and Li settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hangzhou">Hangzhou</a>, completing his Catalogue of Inscriptions on Metal and Stone and writing an afterword on their life. Only about a hundred of her poems survive, mostly in the ci form, her later work marked by loss and nostalgia.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The lyrics of the Song dynasty, by poets such as Su Shi and <b>Li Qingzhao</b>, and the prose essays of writers such as Han Yu and Ouyang Xiu, who revived an “ancient style” of clear and vigorous prose, are classics still read today.</blockquote><a href="/language/classical-chinese">Classical Chinese · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>Educated mothers taught their sons, and <b>Li Qingzhao</b> became one of China’s greatest poets.</blockquote><a href="/societies/song-china">Social Hierarchy of Song China</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Zhangqiu District, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hangzhou">Hangzhou</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36.76,117.49&amp;year=1130" aria-label="Zhangqiu District on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7499 839 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Li Qingzhao"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7949 1109h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7949 1109h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=36.76,117.49&amp;year=1130">What was here in 1130? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
